**ITEM 4 — CLASSROOM TECHNOLOGY REFRESH · STAFF REPORT**

Staff have completed the evaluation of the proposed classroom technology refresh covering 42 instructional spaces in the Science and Humanities buildings. {{a|Total project cost is estimated at $410,000, to be drawn from the technology reserve.}} Replacement would proceed in two phases beginning in the spring term, {{b|with Phase 1 covering 18 rooms in the Science building.}}

The existing equipment was installed between seven and nine years ago. {{c|Service calls on these units have risen by roughly a third over the last two years,}} and replacement parts for the projection units are no longer manufactured.

Three vendors were evaluated. {{d|Meridian AV scored highest on combined total cost and service response time.}} {{e|Meridian AV is also the only one of the three vendors that does not currently hold a state contract, which means selection would require a separate competitive solicitation and adds an estimated ten to fourteen weeks before any equipment could be ordered.}}

{{f|The technology reserve currently holds $455,000. Drawing $410,000 would leave the reserve at approximately $45,000 against a stated floor of $150,000, and staff note this would leave no capacity for emergency replacement during the next two fiscal years.}}

Staff further note that {{g|the Humanities building is included in the deferred maintenance study currently underway, with preliminary findings expected in April.}}

{{h|Accordingly, staff recommend that the committee defer action on the full refresh and instead approve Phase 1 only, at a cost of $175,000, pending the outcome of the deferred maintenance study and confirmation of the reserve floor with Finance.}}

{{i|A full refresh in a single phase is not recommended at this time.}}
